content.text:
Now part 4b: splat/glow helpers, entity passes (stars, trees, statics, glows, petals, fireflies, character), shadow, blit.

Then main loop, input, audio, init.

Splat helper:
```js
function splat(wx,wy,wz, cr,cg,cb, rad, alpha){
  const dx=wx-camX, dy=wy-camY, dz=wz-camZ;
  const zc = dx*F0+dy*F1+dz*F2;
  if (zc < 0.35) return;
  const inv = 1/zc;
  const sx = cxc + (dx*R0+dy*R1+dz*R2)*inv*projX;
  const sy = cyc - (dx*U0+dy*U1+dz*U2)*inv*projY;
  let x0 = Math.floor(sx-rad), x1 = Math.ceil(sx+rad), y0 = Math.floor(sy-rad), y1 = Math.ceil(sy+rad);
  if (x1<0||y1<0||x0>=GW||y0>=GH) return;
  if (x0<0)x0=0; if(y0<0)y0=0; if(x1>GW-1)x1=GW-1; if(y1>GH-1)y1=GH-1;
  const rc = rad+0.55;
  for (let y=y0;y<=y1;y++){
    const ddy = y-sy;
    for (let x=x0;x<=x1;x++){
      const ddx = x-sx, d2 = Math.sqrt(ddx*ddx+ddy*ddy);
      if (d2 > rc) continue;
      let a = alpha*(1 - d2/rc);
      if (a < 0.02) continue;
      const o = y*GW+x;
      if (zc > depth[o]) continue;
      if (a >= 0.985){ colR[o]=cr; colG[o]=cg; colB[o]=cb; depth[o]=zc; }
      else {
        const ia = 1-a;
        colR[o]=colR[o]*ia+cr*a; colG[o]=colG[o]*ia+cg*a; colB[o]=colB[o]*ia+cb*a;
        depth[o]=depth[o]*ia+zc*a;
      }
    }
  }
}
```
Fog for splats: fold fog into the color before calling (compute distance ≈ zc, apply fog there). I'll do fog global: for world entities, compute `f = smoothstep(FOG0,FOG1,zc)` and mix. But zc is camera-forward depth, while the fog distance should be the actual distance. Close enough (use zc). I'll compute fog inside splat? Better: pass an already-fogged color. Since the caller knows the distance... it doesn't cheaply. Let me just have splat apply fog using zc: 

```
const fog = smoothstep(FOG0,FOG1,zc);
if (fog>0.001){ cr=lerp(cr,FOG_R,fog); ... }
```
Only compute when zc > FOG0. Good — automatic and cheap.

glow (additive):
```js
function glow(wx,wy,wz, cr,cg,cb, rad, str){
  ... same projection ...
  const att = str*(1 - d2/rc);
  add with depth test (zc < depth[o])
}
```

Stars: they're at infinity. I'll write a dedicated function `splatDir(dx,dy,dz,...)` — but rotation of the star dirs: apply skyRot around the Y axis per frame.

Actually simpler: treat stars as points at a big distance: worldPos = camPos + starDir*5000. Then zc = dot(dir*5000, F) = 5000*dot(dir,F). If zc<0.35 → cull. Projection: xc/zc = dot(dir,R)/dot(dir,F) — exactly the same as the directional projection. depth = 5000*cos... hmm, using splat with huge coordinates loses precision but it's fine for floats. But the depth test: stars should be behind the mountains. Using distance 5000 → depth 5000 > 9000? No: the mountain depth is 9000 and the moon's is 8000. So star depth = 5000 < 8000/9000 → stars would win! Bad. Use star distance 20000 → depth 20000 > 9000 → culled by the mountains and the moon. But then the sky cells (depth 1e9) let the stars through. 

So: worldPos = camPos + dir*20000, splat with rad 0, alpha 0.9, color = starColor*b. Fog must NOT be applied to stars — at distance 20000, fog would be full → all stars become fog color! So I need a flag. Let me add a `noFog` variant: write `splatNoFog` or pass a `fogOn` boolean parameter. I'll add a parameter `f` (fog amount precomputed by the caller); if negative, skip fog. Cleaner: `splat(..., alpha, fogAmount)` where fogAmount defaults to -1 meaning auto.

Let me define splat(wx,wy,wz,cr,cg,cb,rad,alpha,fogAmt) where fogAmt = -1 → auto compute.

Petals/character/trees: auto fog.

Trees: per-tree, compute the sway, then loop the points, splat each. To reduce cost, skip the fog computation per point by precomputing per-tree fog and lighting, then per point just multiply. But splat applies fog per point. Instead: per point, apply light per tree, then splat with auto fog. Fine.

Tree culling: compute the tree center's camera-space zc; if zc < 3 and it's way off to the side, skip. Simple: if zc < -6 → skip; if zc > FOG1+20 → skip; also if |sxc - cxc| > GW*1.4 → skip.

Trees: also apply the moon lighting to the colors: I baked albedo; multiply by a per-tree light factor: lit = 0.35 + 0.75*max(0,dot(-moonDirFromTree?)). Simplify: use a fixed-direction lighting: the canopy's top-facing points are brighter. Since I baked `shade` per point already (inner darker), I'll additionally tint the whole tree by a warm moonlight factor and darken by distance. Let's compute per tree: `const tl = 0.75 + 0.5*Math.max(0, (tree.x*...)`. Simplest: tl = 1.0 constant, plus fog. Add a slight warm tint for the near-moon-facing side: use the tree's horizontal direction vs the moon azimuth: face = 0.75+0.35*max(0,cos(azTree - MOON_AZ)). Good enough for the blossoms.

Blossom glow: I'll add a soft additive glow at each of the near trees' canopy centers with a low strength pink, to give a bloom feel. Let's add it only for the 6 nearest trees with strength 0.10, radius 3 → subtle.

Statics (torii, lanterns): static points, cull by bounding box? Just loop all (~4000 points) — fine.

Petals: loop 340, update positions, splat with size 0 or 1. Colors pink with slight variation.

Fireflies: additive glow with pulsing.

Character: transform points, splat. Also collision-free.

Shadow: loop over all cells where depth < 1e8 (ground cells) and hash check gwx/gwz within the player radius → darken. Actually let's do it more cheaply: only need to darken the cells whose gw pos is within ~1.0 of the player. Iterating all 27k cells with a hypot each is ~27k sqrt — cheap (0.2ms). But we must also skip sky cells (gwx=0, gwz=0 might accidentally be near the player if the player is at (0,0)... but the player starts at (2,-2) so a ground/sky flag is needed. Use depth < 1e8 to detect ground cells (sky cells have depth 1e9). Good.

Shadow: radius 0.9 around the player, soft: f = 1 - smoothstep(0.35, 0.95, d) → multiply color by (1 - 0.55*f). Note the moon casts the shadow offset in the moon direction; let's offset the shadow center by -moonDir_horizontal*0.35. Nice.

Blit: as designed.

Then the main loop:
```js
let last = performance.now(), T = 0, frames=0, fps=0, fpsAcc=0;
function loop(now){
  const dt = Math.min(0.05, (now-last)/1000); last = now; T += dt;
  update(dt, T);
  render(T);
  ...
}
```

update(): input → camera/player.

Let me write the movement code:
```js
const keys = {};
let camYaw = -0.82, camPitch = 0.16, camDist = 6.6;
let facing = camYaw;   // player facing (yaw)
let walkPhase = 0, moveAmt = 0;
```
Movement: forward = (sin(camYaw), 0, cos(camYaw)); right = (cos(camYaw),0,-sin(camYaw)).
Wait — need consistency with the camera basis: F = (sin(yaw)cos(pitch), sin(pitch), cos(yaw)cos(pitch)) is the direction the camera looks (from the camera toward the target). The camera's horizontal "forward" (toward the target) = (sin(camYaw), cos(camYaw)) ... and the camera is positioned behind the target at target + dist*(sin(camYaw)cos(pitch), sin(pitch), cos(camYaw)cos(pitch)). So the camera looks along -(that offset) = the direction (-sin(camYaw), ., -cos(camYaw)). Hmm, then the camera's forward F = -(offset normalized). Let me define it that way: 
- offset = (sin(camYaw)*cos(camPitch), sin(camPitch), cos(camYaw)*cos(camPitch))
- camPos = target + offset*dist
- F = -offset (normalized — it already is a unit vector) → F0 = -sin(camYaw)cos(camPitch), etc.
- R: perpendicular horizontal: R = (cos(camYaw)? ) Let's recompute with the earlier formula but yaw' = camYaw+π. Using the formula F = (sin(y)cos(p), sin(p), cos(y)cos(p)) and R = (cos(y),0,-sin(y)), U = (-sin(y)sin(p), cos(p), -cos(y)sin(p)): substituting y = camYaw+π gives sin(y)=-sin(camYaw), cos(y)=-cos(camYaw), sin(p) → -sin(camPitch) for F's y and U's y = cos(-camPitch)=cos(camPitch). 

So with y = camYaw + π:
F = (-sin(camYaw)cos(camPitch), -sin(camPitch), -cos(camYaw)cos(camPitch))
R = (-cos(camYaw), 0, sin(camYaw))
U = (sin(camYaw)sin(camPitch), cos(camPitch), cos(camYaw)sin(camPitch))

With camPitch > 0 meaning the camera is above looking down: F.y = -sin(camPitch) < 0 ✓ looking down.

Then the player's "forward" direction on the ground = (-sin(camYaw), -cos(camYaw)) (the horizontal part of F). Let me call fwd = normalize(-sin camYaw, -cos camYaw) = (-sin, -cos). And the right = R horizontal = (-cos camYaw, sin camYaw)? R = (-cos, 0, sin) → the screen-right direction. OK.

Movement: dir = fwd*inputZ + right*inputX where inputZ = +1 for W (forward), inputX = +1 for D (right). Then normalize, move with speed.

The player's facing yaw: for the character model, rotate around Y by `rot` such that the model's local +z maps to the move direction. Model local +z forward. After a rotation by angle a around Y: (x,z) → (x cos a + z sin a, -x sin a + z cos a). Local +z = (0,0,1) maps to (sin a, cos a). So we need sin a = dirX, cos a = dirZ → a = atan2(dirX, dirZ). Good: rot = atan2(dirX, dirZ).

Camera target: the player's position + (0, 1.35, 0). Then camPos = target + offset*camDist, clamped above the terrain.

Camera collision: shrink camDist if the terrain rises. Implement:
```
let d = camDist;
for (let k=0;k<9;k++){
  const px = tx + offset0*d, py = ty + offset1*d, pz = tz + offset2*d;
  if (py > hAt(px,pz)+0.85) break;
  d -= camDist/9;
}
```
Also clamp the final camera Y ≥ hAt+0.7.

Player collision with water: 
```
const nx2 = playerX + dx, nz2 = playerZ + dz;
const h2 = hAt(nx2,nz2);
if (h2 > WATER - 0.35 && Math.hypot(nx2,nz2) < 105) { playerX = nx2; playerZ = nz2; }
else { try sliding: only X, then only Z }
```
playerY = lerp toward hAt (smooth), or directly = h. Direct is fine but jitter on bumpy ground; use a lerp with factor 1-exp(-dt*12).

Also the player should follow the terrain so the character stands on the ground.

Bob: bob = sin(walkPhase*2)*0.045*moveAmt; lean forward: rotate the whole model slightly? Skip lean; just bob and leg swing.

Input:
- keydown/keyup map.
- Q/E: camYaw += ±dt*1.6; R/F: camPitch ±.
- Mouse: pointer lock → movementX → camYaw -= movementX*0.0026; movementY → camPitch += movementY*0.0018 (clamped -0.25..0.62). Note camPitch>0 = the camera above.
Wait: moving the mouse up (movementY negative) should look up → the camera should move down → camPitch decreases. So camPitch += movementY*0.0018 → moving the mouse up (negative) decreases camPitch ✓ (camera lower → looking up).
- Wheel: camDist = clamp(camDist + deltaY*0.004, 2.5, 14).

HUD: fps + quality + moon phase text. Update ~4 times/s.

Also idle "look around" when not moving? Chill: slowly drift the camera yaw when idle for a dreamy effect. Maybe a very slow auto-orbit when the player is idle for >5s. Nice touch: after 6s idle, camYaw += dt*0.03 with an ease-in. Let's add it — "chill" cinematic drift. And stop on input.

Audio: as designed.

Init: bake world, build forest, shrine, stars, weather, character, resize, start the loop. Baking takes maybe 200-400ms; show a "loading" state? It's fine — the start overlay is already up. I'll bake on the first click (after the overlay) instead of at load? Simpler: bake at script load (before the click). The overlay shows immediately (it's in the HTML) so the page will appear then freeze briefly. Fine.

FPS/perf: if fps < 30 for a while, auto-degrade quality. Nice: add adaptive quality (drop one preset if fps<28 for 2s, raise if >55 for 5s). Let's include it but only downward automatically... Let's do it both ways with hysteresis, capped to the user's chosen preset as the max. Actually keep it simple: auto-degrade only, and reset the cap when the user presses 1/2/3.

Let me now write part 4b.

For the character transform, define a helper that splats a part with a pivot rotation:

```js
function drawPart(pts, rot, bob, swing, pivotY, pivotZ, lightMul){
  const cs = Math.cos(rot), sn = Math.sin(rot);
  const ss = Math.sin(swing), sc = Math.cos(swing);
  for (let i=0;i<pts.length;i+=6){
    let x = pts[i], y = pts[i+1]-pivotY, z = pts[i+2]-pivotZ;
    // swing about X axis (legs/arms)
    const y2 = y*sc - z*ss, z2 = y*ss + z*sc;
    y = y2+pivotY; z = z2+pivotZ;
    // yaw
    const wx = playerX + (x*cs + z*sn);
    const wz = playerZ + (-x*sn + z*cs);
    const wy = playerY + y + bob;
    splat(wx,wy,wz, pts[i+3]*lightMul, pts[i+4]*lightMul, pts[i+5]*lightMul, radFor(wy), 0.95);
  }
}
```
where radFor(wy) gives a radius based on distance for near points: rad = clamp(1.6/dist ...). Let me compute the distance from the camera inside splat? I can compute per point: dx,dy,dz from the camera, dist = hypot. That's a sqrt per point (~700 points for the character, 25k for the trees!). For trees, use a per-tree radius (based on the tree center distance) — fine. For the character, do a per-part radius (all parts are at a similar distance). So pass `rad` as a parameter computed by the caller. Good, keeps splat cheap.

Character radius: dist ≈ camDist 6.6 → rad = clamp(2.2/dist*2, 0, 1.6)? Let's compute: at dist 6.6, one cell ≈ 6.6*tan(value)... cell angular size = 1/projX rad ≈ tanH/(GW/2) = 0.806/130 = 0.0062 rad → 0.041 world units at 6.6 → the character (1.75 tall) spans ~43 cells. Radius 0.5-1 is fine. Let's set rad = clamp(3.2/dist, 0, 1.4) → at 6.6 → 0.48. Good.

Tree radius: at distance 40, one cell ≈ 0.25 world units → a blossom cluster point with rad 0 gives a sparse canopy; use rad = clamp(6/dist, 0.25, 2.2) → at 10 → 0.6; at 40 → 0.25. Hmm, for far trees each point covering <1 cell means only some points land on cells → a sparse, speckled canopy (which actually looks like an airy blossom tree, fine). Let's use rad = clamp(7/dist, 0.1, 2.4).

OK, and alpha 0.85 for canopy points.

Statics: radius clamp(5/dist,0.1,1.5).

Now write. Also the tree loop must be skipped if fogged out: if the tree's center distance > FOG1+15 skip.

Let me write part 4b now (entity passes + shadow + blit).

For the sky rotation of stars, define skyRot += dt*0.0006 (very slow) — actually since the whole sky rotates, the moon should rotate too, but I've fixed the moon. A mismatched motion is only noticeable over minutes; keep the star rotation extremely slow (0.0004 rad/s → 0.024 rad/min) — imperceptible in a short session, gives life over time. Fine. Rotation: rotate the star dir around the world Y axis by skyRot. Also rotate the moon's direction? For consistency I'd have to recompute the moon dir and the tangent basis — doable but the MOON_* consts are used everywhere. Skip: set the star rotation to 0.0002 rad/s (a barely-there drift).

Hmm, actually a mismatch would be visible if the moon sets... it never sets. Fine.

