Wait, my fastAz implementation is sketchy. Let me write a proper correct version.

The standard fast atan2 approximation (from the "atan2 approximation" by Jim Shima / commonly used):

```
function atan2Approx(y, x) {
  const absY = Math.abs(y) + 1e-10;
  let r, angle;
  if (x >= 0) {
    r = (x - absY) / (x + absY);
    angle = 0.1963*r*r - 0.9817*r + Math.PI/4;
  } else {
    r = (x + absY) / (absY - x);
    angle = 0.1963*r*r - 0.9817*r + 3*Math.PI/4;
  }
  return y < 0 ? -angle : angle;
}
```
This is the well-known approximation with max error ~0.0038 rad. Let me use exactly this (no Math.PI/4 constants computed per call — hoist them as consts).

Let me replace my fastAz with this correct version.

Also I should double check: my call is `fastAz(hz2, hx)` intending az = atan2(z_component, x_component) i.e., y=hz2, x=hx ✓ matches the original `Math.atan2(hz2, hx)`. ✓

Note: MOON_AZ is compared against `az` via `Math.cos(az - MOON_AZ)` — with the approximation the error is tiny ✓.

Let me fix fastAz.

